-ICA verdict is READY (5 AVAILABLE / 1 DERIVABLE / 0 MISSING) with no blocking gap, so priority is not discounted by open unknowns.\\n\\nEffort: Small \u2014 see Effort section.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Benefits\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-20T21:30:39.038127Z</sub>\\n\\n- Agents converge to the correct narrow query faster instead of defaulting to full-content loads, reducing wasted context-window spend across every `backlog_view()` call.\\n- Agents that hit an over-budget response after narrowing are pointed at a mechanism (`navigate=`/`head=`/`skip_tokens=`) that can actually resolve their state, instead of being told to repeat an action already exhausted \u2014 removing a retry-loop failure mode.\\n- Brings `backlog_core/server.py` into compliance with R7 (`agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md`), completing the operationalization of the normative requirements PR #3056 established, and unblocks parent item #3054.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Research\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-20T09:14:32.531697Z</sub>\\n\\n## Wave 0 Technical Research \u2014 hint-construction convention\\n\\n### Established pattern: `next_call`\\n\\nThree call sites in `backlog_core/` and `sam_schema/` build a machine-actionable continuation\\nhint under the literal key `next_call`. All three share the same shape:\\n\\n1. **`backlog_core/disclosure_handler.py` `_handle_extract()` (around line 484-490)** \u2014 the\\n   pattern the item's context already cites as canonical:\\n   ```python\\n   next_call: str | None = None\\n   if bounded.truncated:\\n       next_skip = skip_tokens + bounded.returned_tokens\\n       next_call = (\\n           f'backlog_view(selector=\\\"{selector}\\\", navigate=\\\"{ordinal}\\\", '\\n           f\\\"head={head_tokens}, skip_tokens={next_skip})\\\"\\n       )\\n   ```\\n2. **`backlog_core/server.py` `backlog_list()` (around line 2267-2268)**:\\n   ```python\\n   if has_more:\\n       response[\\\"next_call\\\"] = f\\\"backlog_list(offset={offset + effective_limit}, limit={effective_limit})\\\"\\n   ```\\n3. **`sam_schema/server.py` `_paginate_results()` (around line 175-177)**, a shared helper used\\n   by both `sam_plan` and `sam_task` list/ready actions:\\n   ```python\\n   if has_more:\\n       next_offset = offset + len(page)\\n       result.next_call = f\\\"{tool_name}(offset={next_offset}, limit={effective_limit})\\\"\\n   ```\\n\\nCommon shape across all three (this is the convention a fix must match):\\n\\n- **Field name**: `next_call`, always a bare string (or `None`/absent), never nested under a\\n  prose paragraph.\\n- **Conditional, not unconditional**: the field is populated only when a next step is genuinely\\n  actionable (`bounded.truncated`, `has_more`). When there is nothing left to fetch, the field is\\n  omitted/`None` \u2014 the hint never recommends an action the caller has already exhausted (this is\\n  literally R7's second clause).\\n- **Content is a literal, copy-pasteable call expression**: `tool_name(param=value, ...)`, not a\\n  sentence describing what to do. The reader can paste it directly.\\n- **Parameters cite exactly the current response's state**: the *cumulative* position is computed\\n  from values already in scope at the call site \u2014 `skip_tokens + bounded.returned_tokens`,\\n  `offset + effective_limit`, `offset + len(page)` \u2014 not a static default. The caller never has to\\n  compute the next offset itself.\\n- **One suggestion, not several**: each of the three sites emits exactly one `next_call` string,\\n  matching R7's \\\"never recommend retrieving everything as its first suggestion\\\" \u2014 there is no\\n  competing alternative offered.\\n\\n### The two violating call sites do not follow this convention\\n\\n`_build_compact_manifest()` (`backlog_core/server.py` around 2275-2314) and\\n`_build_over_budget_view()` (around 2352-2392) both diverge from every element of the pattern\\nabove:\\n\\n- Different field names (`_hint`, `_usage`) instead of `next_call`.\\n- Content is prose containing two-to-three alternative calls as literal strings embedded in a\\n  multi-line human-readable message, not a single machine-actionable expression.\\n- Unconditional: both functions always populate the field on every call, regardless of what the\\n  caller has already requested. `_build_compact_manifest`'s `_hint` recommends \\\"Load full content\\\"\\n  as its *first* line unconditionally \u2014 this is the literal violation of R7's first clause (\\\"must\\n  never recommend retrieving everything as its first suggestion\\\").\\n- No use of already-known caller state: at the `_build_over_budget_view` call site\\n  (`server.py` around line 2681, inside the `if not summary:` branch), `sections_filter` (set at\\n  line 2633 from the `sections` param) and `section` (the raw param) are both in scope and\\n  non-None whenever the caller already narrowed the request \u2014 but neither is passed into\\n  `_build_over_budget_view`, so the returned `_usage` string always suggests `sections=`/`section=`\\n  usage even to a caller who is already using it. This is the literal violation of R7's second\\n  clause (\\\"must never recommend an action the caller has already exhausted\\\").\\n\\n### R7 normative text (`docs/agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md`, \\\"R7 \u2014 Hints must be\\nactionable\\\")\\n\\n> A response that withholds content states what the caller must do to obtain it, using addresses\\n> valid in that same response. A hint must never recommend retrieving everything as its first\\n> suggestion, never recommend an action the caller has already exhausted, and never name a\\n> mechanism absent from the response it accompanies.\\n\\nR6 (same doc) explicitly cross-references R7's hint as the mechanism for reaching paginated\\ncontent: \\\"the caller reaches the remainder with a same-tool page request named by R7's hint.\\\"\\n\\n### Prior art: no ADR governs hint *string construction* specifically\\n\\nNo ADR or PR title matching \\\"hint\\\" was found scoped to the construction convention itself \u2014 R7 in\\nthe consumption contract doc is the only normative spec, and the `next_call` pattern above is\\nimplicit convention (established by the three working call sites and their tests), not\\ndocumented separately. The fix should express its `next_call` construction convention inline in\\ncode (docstring/comment), consistent with how `_handle_extract()` already documents its own\\n`next_call` semantics.\\n\\n### Existing test coverage (prior art for what a fix's tests must add)\\n\\n`tests/test_backlog_core_server.py` already asserts *fidelity* (the hint embeds the caller's exact\\nselector) for both violating sites, but not R7 compliance:\\n\\n- `test_backlog_view_summary_true_hint_contains_selector` (around line 1046) \u2014 asserts `_hint`\\n  contains the exact selector string, nothing about suggestion ordering.\\n- `test_build_over_budget_view_usage_mentions_sections_param` (around line 3898) \u2014 asserts `_usage`\\n  contains `sections=` and the selector, nothing about conditioning on prior narrowing.\\n\\nNeither existing test would catch the R7 violations described above (first-suggestion ordering,\\nalready-exhausted advice) \u2014 a fix needs new tests asserting: (1) `_hint`'s first suggestion is\\nsection-scoped when `sections_index` is present, not full-content load, and (2) `_usage`/an\\nequivalent field omits or reorders the sections-narrowing suggestion when the caller already\\nsupplied `section`/`sections`.\\n\\n### Recurrence assessment\\n\\nConfirmed two call sites need the fix (`_build_compact_manifest`, `_build_over_budget_view`); no\\nthird violating site exists \u2014 every other hint-construction call site in `backlog_core/` and\\n`sam_schema/` (`backlog_list`, `_handle_extract`, `_paginate_results`) already follows the\\n`next_call` convention correctly.\\n\\n</div>\\n\\n## Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-20T21:21:51.090823Z</sub>\\n\\n## Design Intent Alignment\\n\\nAlignment assessment: **ALIGNED**\\n\\n### Concerns\\n\\n| Category | Proposed Change Excerpt | Mission Source Violated | Citation |\\n|----------|------------------------|-------------------------|----------|\\n| \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 |\\n\\n### Summary\\n\\n0 concerns identified \u2014 proposed change is consistent with product mission and historical direction.\\n\\n**Rationale**: The fix updates runtime hint strings in `backlog_core/server.py` (`_build_compact_manifest()` and `_build_over_budget_view()`) to comply with R7 (\\\"Hints must be actionable\\\") from the **Agent Markdown Consumption \u2014 Behaviour Specification** (`docs/agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md`:159-164). This requirement was established as normative via PR #3056 (merged 2026-08-20), which added the component architecture map and expanded the markdown consumption contract with design decisions R1\u2013R8.\\n\\n**Mission alignment**: The development-harness plugin's stated purpose is to \\\"orchestrate feature development through a structured 7-stage pipeline\\\" with agents as the primary consumer. Ensuring that agent-facing runtime hints comply with normative markdown-consumption requirements is core to that mission \u2014 hints are read \\\"on every call, at the moment of the decision\\\" (backlog item description) and directly steer agent behavior.\\n\\n**Design principle alignment**: The contract (`agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md:1-3`) explicitly states \\\"Every consumer of every operation described here is an AI agent\\\" and R7 mandates that hints \\\"never recommend retrieving everything as its first suggestion, never recommend an action the caller has already exhausted, and never name a mechanism absent from the response it accompanies.\\\" This fix ensures `backlog_core` follows the architectural requirement that agent-facing behavior comply with the markdown consumption contract. Component architecture (`component-architecture.md`) assigns `backlog_core` responsibility to consume and respect normative requirements from the markdown contract.\\n\\n**Historical direction**: PR #3056 (merged 2026-08-20) closed \\\"gaps a six-reviewer pass found in the markdown consumption contract\\\" and explicitly recorded R6\u2013R8 as normative. This fix (#3060) operationalizes one of those requirements (R7) in the live codebase.\\n\\n**Reference implementation**: The fix follows the established pattern from `disclosure_handler.py` (lines 487-490), which correctly constructs hints that reference mechanisms present in the response structure.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] No hint recommends retrieving everything as its first suggestion, per R7\\n- [ ] The over-budget hint offers an action a narrowed caller has not already taken\\n- [ ] Hints name only mechanisms present in the response they accompany\\n- [ ] Addresses appearing in a hint are valid in that same response\\n</div>\\n\\n## Expected Behavior\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-20T21:31:56.200066Z</sub>\\n\\nWhen `backlog_view(summary=True)` (the default) returns a compact manifest, the `_hint` field's first recommended call must be a narrowing action (`section=`/`sections=`) \u2014 an unqualified full-content load (`summary=False` with no section) must not appear as the first suggestion, and may appear only as a subordinate/fallback option after narrower ones.\\n\\nWhen `backlog_view()` returns an over-budget response (`_over_budget: true`), the `_usage` field must not recommend the same `section=`/`sections=` narrowing the caller already applied to produce that exact response. It must name the token-bounded pagination mechanism that actually exists in this package (`navigate=<ordinal>`, `head=<tokens>`, `skip_tokens=<offset>`) as the applicable next call for extracting a windowed slice of an oversized section \u2014 matching the pattern already implemented in `disclosure_handler.py`'s `next_call` hint.\\n\\nIn both cases, every parameter name and address named in the hint text must correspond to a mechanism and value actually present in the response object it accompanies (R7's third prohibition) \u2014 e.g. an ordinal named in a hint must appear in that same response's `sections_index`.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Reproducibility\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-20T21:27:30.937900Z</sub>\\n\\n1. Call `backlog_view(selector='<any item with a non-trivial body>', summary=True)` (the default mode) and inspect the returned `_hint` field. Its first line reads `Load full content: backlog_view(selector='{selector}', summary=False)` \u2014 an unqualified full-content load is recommended before any narrower option (verified: `backlog_core/server.py:2306-2309`).\\n2. Call `backlog_view(selector='<item>', summary=False, section='<a single section that is itself >4000 tokens>')`. The narrowed request is still over budget, so the response falls into the over-budget branch. Inspect the returned `_usage` field \u2014 it recommends `sections=[...]`, `section='0,1,3'`, or `section='/regex/'`, the exact narrowing action the caller already performed to produce this response, and it never mentions `navigate=`, `head=`, or `skip_tokens=` (verified: `backlog_core/server.py:2379-2387`).\\n3. Compare both strings against `disclosure_handler.py`'s `_handle_extract()` `next_call` hint (`backlog_core/disclosure_handler.py:487-490`), which only emits a `skip_tokens=`-based continuation and never recommends an exhausted or absent action \u2014 this is the in-package pattern the two violating hints deviate from.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Priority**: P1 \u2014 small change, disproportionate effect on agent behaviour\\n- Intended behaviour is defined in `plugins/development-harness/docs/agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md`. Component ownership is defined in `plugins/development-harness/docs/component-architecture.md`. Parent: #3054.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Root-Cause Analysis\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-20T09:16:26.881207Z</sub>\\n\\n**Root Cause**: Hints in `_build_compact_manifest()` and `_build_over_budget_view()` were written as freeform guidance without reference to the R7 normative requirement or consultation with the disclosure_handler.py reference implementation.\\n\\n**Evidence Chain**:\\n- R7 requirement (agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md:159-164) is explicit: hints must never recommend full retrieval first, never recommend actions already exhausted, and never name mechanisms absent from the response\\n- _build_compact_manifest (server.py:2306-2309): _hint field leads with \\\"Load full content: summary=False\\\", violating R7 clause \\\"never recommend retrieving everything as its first suggestion\\\"\\n- _build_over_budget_view (server.py:2379-2387): _usage field recommends section= selection but does not document navigate= + skip_tokens= parameters, violating R7 clause \\\"never name a mechanism absent from the response it accompanies\\\"\\n- disclosure_handler.py:487-490 demonstrates correct pattern: next_call hint uses navigate=\\\"{ordinal}\\\" (section address) with head={head_tokens} and skip_tokens={next_skip}, all parameters present in BoundedResponse structure being returned\\n- Root-cause mechanism: independent hint construction without consulting R7 requirement or reference implementation left two response-building functions generating incompatible guidance\\n\\n**Fix Scope**:\\n- Rewrite _hint in _build_compact_manifest to recommend section selection first (using section= or sections= parameters) and defer full-content retrieval to an alternative, not a primary action\\n- Rewrite _usage in _build_over_budget_view to name navigate=, head=, and skip_tokens= parameters as primary pagination mechanism, matching the sections_index discovery already included in the response\\n- Ensure both hints reference only mechanisms and addresses present in the response object they accompany (R7 requirement)\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"d4d9098c132e858c3cd17840c6f96c97a4e044e79b9e9f5f8064878eb849b463\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABP5mR4Q\"}"}